      "backgroundContext": "The practice of holding three distinct sessions of Parliament is a well-established convention in India, though not explicitly written in the Constitution. Historically, the Constituent Assembly debated the frequency of sessions, with Dr. B.R. Ambedkar emphasizing the need for frequent sittings to keep the executive accountable. Over the decades, the average number of sitting days has declined from over 120 days in the 1950s to around 60-70 days annually in recent years. The transition to the new Parliament building represents a physical and symbolic shift, addressing space constraints and technological limitations of the colonial-era structure built in 1927.",
